[80.314045, "o", "\u001b[0m$ \u001b[0mcd /tmp/opencode/src/stp && sed -n '1,220p' README.markdown 2>/dev/null || ls; echo \"---cmake opts---\"; grep -n \"option(\" CMakeLists.txt | head -60; echo \"---deps docs---\"; ls docs; grep -rn \"Boost\\|boost\\|gmp\\|GMP\\|minisat\\|cryptominisat\" docs/*.md* 2>/dev/null | head -40\r\n[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT)\r\n[![CI](https://github.com/stp/stp/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/stp/stp/actions/workflows/ci.yml)\r\n\r\n# STP\r\n\r\nSTP is a constraint solver (or SMT solver) for the quantifier-free theories of bitvectors, arrays, floating-point and linear real arithmetic. These types of constraints are generated by program analysis tools, theorem provers, automated bug finders, cryptographic attack tools, intelligent fuzzers, model checkers, and by many other applications.\r\n\r\nComprehensive details are provided in the manual and homepage: https://stp.github.io/\r\n\r\n## Build and install\r\n\r\nFor a quick install:\r\n\r\n```\r\nsudo apt-get install git build-essential cmake bison flex python3 \\\r\n                     libgmp-dev pkg-config zlib1g-dev\r\ngit clone https://github.com/stp/stp\r\ncd stp\r\n./configure.sh --auto-download\r\ncmake --build build -j$(nproc)\r\nsudo cmake --install build\r\n```\r\n\r\n[CryptoMiniSat](https://github.com/msoos/cryptominisat) is the backend STP solves with by default, and `--auto-download` covers it like every other dependency: STP clones and builds [stp/cryptominisat](https://github.com/stp/cryptominisat) at a pinned commit that carries the IPASIR-UP propagator interface the linear-arithmetic theory drives. A CryptoMiniSat without the interface still builds STP; the theory then runs full-lazy on it, configure warns about it, and `--cadical` restores propagation for a run. An installed one is found and preferred. `libgmp-dev`, `pkg-config` and `zlib1g-dev` are its packages, not STP's.\r\n\r\n[CaDiCaL](https://github.com/arminbiere/cadical) is compiled in alongside it, and is what a build without CryptoMiniSat solves with. `--cryptominisat`, `--cadical` or `--minisat` selects a compiled-in backend for one run.\r\n\r\nIncremental [Kissat](https://github.com/stp/kissat/tree/stp) is an optional\r\nbackend. Configure STP with `--kissat --auto-download` to build the pinned\r\nfork, then select it with `stp --kissat input.smt2`. A prebuilt checkout can\r\nbe selected with `--kissat-dir=/path/to/kissat`.\r\nThe [Kissat integration guide](docs/kissat.rst) describes the build options\r\nand incremental API.\r\n\r\nWith `--cadical`, `--cadical-elim=0|1` controls variable elimination, and\r\n`--cadical-elimmineff=N` / `--cadical-elimmaxeff=N` set its minimum and maximum\r\neffort. For example:\r\n\r\n```sh\r\nstp input.smt2 --cadical --cadical-elim=0\r\nstp input.smt2 --cadical --cadical-elimmineff=10000 --cadical-elimmaxeff=100000\r\n```\r\n\r\nUnspecified options retain the backend settings and STP's automatic policies.\r\nExplicit values override the corresponding `CADICAL_*` environment variables\r\nand search-bias settings, survive backend rebuilds, and an explicit\r\n`--cadical-elim=1` keeps elimination enabled when incremental inprocessing is\r\nretired. Invalid values and requests with another backend are rejected before\r\nsolving. Add `-s` to see the effective settings. Effort limits follow CaDiCaL's\r\ncost model; it can raise the allowance for large formulas, so the maximum is\r\nnot a hard resolution-count limit. These are tuning controls with measured\r\ntradeoffs; none of them is set by default.\r\n\r\nSTP builds CryptoMiniSat with `-DNOCADICAL=ON`, so it bundles no CaDiCaL of its own and STP links the revision it pins -- which is what keeps `--cadical-factor` available. That option removes only backbone extraction, which STP never asks for.\r\n\r\nThere are no submodules: `--auto-download` fetches every dependency at a pinned revision and builds it with this build's own compiler and flags. Without it, configuration stops and says what to install or where to point it -- nothing here reaches the network unless it is asked to.\r\n\r\nOr, using [Homebrew](https://brew.sh):\r\n```\r\nbrew ins"]
[80.31413, "o", "tall stp\r\n```\r\n\r\nOr, with Docker, which needs nothing installed but Docker itself and reads the\r\nproblem on standard input:\r\n```\r\ngit clone https://github.com/stp/stp\r\ncd stp\r\ndocker build -t stp .\r\necho \"(set-logic QF_BV)\r\n(assert (= (bvsdiv (_ bv3 2) (_ bv2 2)) (_ bv0 2)))\r\n(check-sat)\r\n(exit)\" | docker run --rm -i stp\r\n```\r\n\r\n[Building STP](https://stp.github.io/stp/building.html) covers the rest: the\r\nconfiguration variables, the SAT backends and how to choose between them,\r\nbuilding against dependencies you have built but not installed, static builds,\r\nand Windows.\r\n\r\n## Usage\r\n\r\nRun with an SMT-LIB2 file, which is the recommended input format:\r\n\r\n```\r\nstp myproblem.smt2\r\n```\r\n\r\nSTP also reads from standard input, as in the Docker example above.\r\n[Running STP](https://stp.github.io/stp/command-line.html) describes the\r\ncommand line: input formats, output and exit status, choosing a SAT solver,\r\nlimits, statistics, and writing CNF.\r\n\r\nOverflowing a 32-bit integer using the Python interface:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nfrom stp import *\r\n\r\nx, y = BitVecs('x y', 32)\r\ns = Solver()\r\ns.add(ULT(x + y, 20), UGT(x, 10), UGT(y, 10))\r\nprint(s.check())  # sat\r\nprint(s.model())  # e.g. [x = 4294967288, y = 11]\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe [manual](https://stp.github.io/stp/) documents the accepted subset of SMT-LIB2,\r\nthe C, C++ and Python API, incremental solving and array extensionality, and how\r\nSTP works. Its sources are in [`docs/`](docs), and\r\n[`docs/README.md`](docs/README.md) says how to build and read it locally.\r\n\r\n## Contributing\r\n\r\n[Source code layout](https://stp.github.io/stp/code-guide.html) describes what lives\r\nwhere, [Testing](https://stp.github.io/stp/testing.html) how to build and run the\r\ntest suite, and [Developer tools](https://stp.github.io/stp/tools.html) the programs\r\nunder `tools/` other than `stp`. STP is written by\r\n[many people](https://stp.github.io/stp/#history-and-authors), who work on it in\r\ntheir own time, or because it helps with their work or study.\r\n---cmake opts---\r\n54:option(SANITIZE \"Use Clang sanitizers\" OFF)\r\n140:# overruling a choice. It used to be a plain option() defaulting ON with an\r\n144:stp_migrate_bool_option(ENABLE_ASSERTIONS)\r\n145:stp_option(ENABLE_ASSERTIONS \"Build with assertions enabled\")\r\n148:    stp_set_option(ENABLE_ASSERTIONS OFF)\r\n150:stp_set_option(ENABLE_ASSERTIONS ON)\r\n188:option(BUILD_SHARED_LIBS \"Build the shared library\" ON)\r\n189:option(STATICCOMPILE \"Compile static library and executable\" OFF)\r\n305:option(SUPPRESS_WARNINGS \"Silence all warnings\" OFF)\r\n592:option(TUNE_NATIVE \"Use -mtune=native\" OFF)\r\n607:option(USE_POPCNT \"Use the POPCNT instruction (requires Nehalem/2008 or later)\" ON)\r\n633:option(ENABLE_LTO \"Build with link-time optimisation\" OFF)\r\n866:option(COVERAGE \"Build with coverage check\" OFF)\r\n871:option(WERROR \"Build with warnings as errors\" OFF)\r\n883:option(USE_THREAD_LOCAL \"Use thread-local storage when available\" ON)\r\n1102:option(BUILD_EXECUTABLES \"Build executables\" ON)\r\n1103:option(STP_BUILD_PARALLEL \"Build the Linux native stp-p executable\" OFF)\r\n1121:option(USE_SATSUMA \"Build optional Satsuma CNF symmetry preprocessing\" OFF)\r\n1131:option(USE_MINISAT \"Use the MiniSat solver backend\" OFF)\r\n1145:option(USE_CADICAL \"Use the CaDiCaL solver backend\" ON)\r\n1168:option(USE_KISSAT \"Use the incremental Kissat solver backend\" OFF)\r\n1556:stp_option(BUILD_MANPAGE \"Build and install the stp(1) manpage with help2man\")\r\n1693:option(ENABLE_HIGHS \"Enable optional HiGHS LP/MIP proposals with exact certification\" OFF)\r\n1694:option(ENABLE_HIGHS_CUT_LOG \"Enable the optional HiGHS 1.12.0 root-cut recipe patch\" OFF)\r\n1772:option(ENABLE_TESTING \"Enable testing\" OFF)\r\n---deps docs---\r\nMakefile\r\nREADME.md\r\n_extra\r\n_static\r\n_templates\r\napi.rst\r\narchitecture.rst\r\narray-extensionality.rst\r\nbenchmark-failures.rst\r\nbenchmarks.rst\r\nbuild.py\r\nbuilding.rst\r\nbv-abstraction.rst\r\nc-api-lifetime.rst\r\ncode-guide.rst\r\ncommand-line.rst\r\nconf.py\r\nfp-abstraction.rst\r\nincremental-solving.rst\r\nindex.rst\r\nkissat.rst\r\nlinear-real-arithmetic.rst\r\nmake.bat\r\nparallel-solving.rst\r\nreference\r\nreleasing.rst\r\nrequirements.txt\r\nsat"]
